\section{Analysis: Entropy-Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:analysis}

The results in \S\ref{sec:experiments} reveal a striking asymmetry: importance-based eviction exhibits $11$--$42\times$ severity amplification in low-sparsity phases, while random eviction remains flat at ${\sim}1\times$ across all phases. Why is compound severity phase-modulated for one strategy but not the other? We propose \emph{entropy-discrimination collapse} as an explanatory mechanism: head pruning redistributes attention mass in a phase-dependent manner, degrading the discriminative power of importance scores precisely in phases where attention is already broadly distributed.

\subsection{Attention Redistribution Under Head Pruning}
\label{sec:attn-redistribution}

When a fraction $s$ of attention heads are pruned, the surviving heads must compensate for the lost information pathways. We characterize this compensation through the attention entropy of a surviving head $h$ at query position $i$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_i^{(h)} = -\sum_{j=1}^{T} a_{ij}^{(h)} \log a_{ij}^{(h)}
    \label{eq:attn-entropy}
\end{equation}
Higher $\mathcal{H}$ indicates more uniformly distributed attention. The effect of pruning on $\mathcal{H}$ depends critically on the reasoning phase.

In the \textbf{high-sparsity phase}, attention is already concentrated on a few key positions ($\mathcal{H}$ is low). Head redundancy is high---concentrated attention patterns are likely to overlap across heads---so pruning removes largely redundant capacity. The surviving heads continue to attend to the same narrow set of positions, and $\mathcal{H}$ changes minimally.

In the \textbf{low-sparsity phase}, attention distributes broadly across many positions ($\mathcal{H}$ is already elevated). Head redundancy is low: each head captures partially non-overlapping information. Pruning forces surviving heads to broaden their attention further to compensate for the lost coverage, pushing $\mathcal{H}$ above its already high baseline.

